WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Adventures in the Magic Kingdom.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:07.840
This is your ticket to breathtaking adventures in the Magic Kingdom.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:31.180
NOSTALGIA And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.300
I'm Mike. And I'm Sean.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.420
Sean, how many parents use this as an excuse to say, you're going to Disney World?

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.220
Oh, I hope few.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.980
It's an evil thing to do, right?

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:54.700
Very evil. But, you know, what's funny is when I was a kid, we had these Disney sing-along tapes, which I'm sure a lot of people grew up around.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:02.280
And it was basically just a collection of famous Disney songs with the actual scenes from the movies, but it would have a little sing-along things.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.420
Everybody could sing-along. I don't know why I'm describing it.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:05.380
You all know what a sing-along is.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:15.880
The point being that they made a special one, not related to scenes from movies, but just from around the Magic Kingdom of Disney World in Orlando, Florida.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:33.640
And that was like, for me, from the age of three to the age of 13, when I finally got to go to Disney World, that was my way of going to Disney World, was putting on that tape, watching those songs, and watching these other kids have fun in Disney World.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:48.580
I don't know why I'm not going to go to Disney World because we didn't have YouTube back then, but I guess now the three-year-old me is on YouTube watching some really snobby person go to hotels in Disney World and review them and say, this is what a five-star Disney World hotel looks like.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.520
Because that's like all the rage now on Disney World YouTube.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.180
My point being, you can't emulate the Magic Kingdom, Disney World, Disneyland.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:10.520
Like, the video game has too tall of a task in front of it to say that we're going to take the fun of Disney World and put it in your Nintendo Entertainment System.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:22.620
Well, on one hand, I wouldn't know because I've never been to Disney World, nor have I been to Disneyland, and I imagine they're similar.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:23.840
Maybe not.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:25.680
Again, wouldn't know.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.500
On the other hand, this is all I had because this is one of the few NES games.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.900
In fact, this was the first NES game I ever played in my life.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.820
Whoa. Yeah.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.200
What a bombshell you just dropped on us.

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:55.500
Yeah, and you know, when I say play, I mean I picked it up, got annoyed with how it kept, like, crashing because there was something wrong with the NES, I'm pretty sure, and then never played it again.

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:59.000
You needed a new 72-pin connector.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:14.620
Probably. But it's crazy how this game, from probably the under 45 minutes that I played it, really did hold a place in my brain of just like, this is just what the NES is.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.180
And it did not paint a very pretty picture.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.720
Well, it's funny that you say that because in a way, right, Disney is huge with children.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.100
The NES is growing with children.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:34.260
It feels natural to have, like, more Disney games on the NES than what we currently have.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.580
Like, we don't have really.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.320
We had, like, a Mickey Mouse game.

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.300
We had the DuckTales game.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.840
I'm sure there's at least one other one I'm forgetting.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:54.260
But, like, this is the first one that really takes, like, Disney as a brand and says, like, hey, for 50 or 60 bucks, like, you can have that same experience as the Disney World kids but at home.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:57.620
I am putting those words in their mouth, though.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:03.320
They aren't really saying this is a substitute for the Disney World experience.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:04.860
I'm projecting that.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:16.860
So let's forget about that for a minute and instead just talk about the premise of the game, that there are six keys that apparently open up the castle at the Magic Kingdom.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:27.940
And when I hear Magic Kingdom, I do think that is in Disney World in Orlando, Florida because that's one of the areas you can go to.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.520
You know, like, there's Epcot.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.920
There's the Animal one.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:33.440
And then there's the Magic Kingdom.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.300
So I'm putting this in Orlando, not in Disneyland.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.840
I think they are placing it in Orlando.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.560
Great. The whole thing, they call them attractions.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.200
It's not, like, a level or world.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:53.460
Like, you're walking around the park and you can see where people get refreshments.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.560
And one of them is, I think, even called, like, Space Mountain or whatever.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.360
So it's definitely Disney World if you're, like, on mushrooms.

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.780
Yeah. There you go.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:13.880
And so apparently I take six keys to open up this castle and Goofy, I guess, left them all behind somewhere scattered throughout.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:15.380
So you now have to.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.060
And also, like, they're not scattered out.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.760
They're actually just at the ride.

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.160
So, like, what's the deal?

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.040
Like, did he purposefully leave them there?

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.160
And now it's up to you to have fun at Disney World by riding the rides in order to get the keys?

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.300
Like, was it a ruse all along?

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.420
I didn't beat the game.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:34.580
So maybe that is the grand reveal that, like, we just wanted you to have a fun day at Disney World.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.460
And now that you did, you have all the keys and you can go inside the castle.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:42.200
That said, I have a gripe right from the beginning of this game.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.960
As soon as you start, okay, you play as some kid that you don't get to design what they look like or whatever.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:55.160
But you do get to put some personal touch on by saying the name of the character that you want to play as.

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:03.000
But all it says on screen is, please enter your name with an image of Goofy on it.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.260
Like, with his hands out.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.500
Goofy telling you to do that.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.700
Please enter your name.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.240
And, yes, you could read it that way.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.440
But you could also read it as, like, am I naming Goofy?

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:13.860
No. Is this some RPG?

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:20.540
Is that what you thought? I'm just saying, is this some kind of RPG where you know the real name of the character, but you can change it.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:25.940
And so I know that that's Goofy, but maybe, like, I can change it so that I'm Mike this time.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:31.560
Please enter your name does imply that you're, you know, you're going to play as yourself.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:37.600
But with Goofy on the screen, it's not clear yet that you're not going to be Goofy for the remainder of the game.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:40.120
You've already found out that he's the one who lost the keys.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.120
I'm going to say real quick that this sounds like a you problem.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.160
Not so much a problem with the game.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.180
Interesting. All right.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.000
And for what it's worth, I didn't think that that was actually going to happen, of course.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:55.080
Oh, sure. I just brought it to you.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.340
No, it's a confusing UI thing.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:01.260
And you know me, I don't, I'm very particular when it comes to the UIs.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:08.960
And so I think that they could have freed that up a little bit by just putting the kid that you're going to be on the screen so that it's clear that's who you're naming.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:16.360
That's just me. We've been ruined by how, like, beat you over the head with it that, like, naming yourself in Pokemon was.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.700
Like, it wasn't Professor Oak saying, what's my name?

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.200
It was, they showed you this kid.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:21.420
Right? So.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:28.840
Well, that can't be me in this game anyway, because that kid is wearing a cowboy hat, and I've never been one to wear a cowboy hat.

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:29.920
Yeah, it's really weird.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.320
Is that a thing that kids used to just wear, like, around?

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:38.420
Like, I've seen movies where there's just little kids either dressed as sailors or dressed as cowboys.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:44.380
Like, I guess you wouldn't know either, Mike, but it just seems like, was that a thing kids did?

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.920
I could understand if the kid that you play as in the game was getting dressed up, depending on, like, what ride he was in, right?

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.760
Like, you change costumes for the different areas or whatever.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:08:01.020
Like, that's fun. But just to be a kid who wears a cowboy hat, that's too bold of a kid, you know?

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.460
Yeah. Like, is that a Mickey Mouse thing?

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.900
Maybe not bold of the kid, or maybe it's more cruel of the parents.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:09.900
And, you know, it's funny, too.

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:17.640
It's like, there's an easy opportunity here to just have him wear the Mickey Mouse ears that they want you to buy at the game park, right?

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:19.980
Like, just have it be like, oh, he's got the Mickey Mouse.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.440
Maybe they weren't around back then, but I'm pretty sure they were.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:35.680
Point being that it just feels like, it feels weird that you can't even just customize the character a little bit because he's such a, I feel like anybody who wears a cowboy hat has a lot of personality and therefore should just be an established character.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.380
I can't argue with that.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.900
But the Magic Kingdom itself, though, looking kind of dull.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.820
Yeah, there's only ever, like, one other person in the park.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:47.620
And then they disappear.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:52.900
Yeah. And they're just there to make you feel bad about not knowing obscure Disney trivia.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.780
And, you know, if you do know that trivia, give you that other key.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.120
But otherwise, yeah, there's mean to you.

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:02.680
I'll just tell you how crazy the trivia is.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.460
Okay. So first off, it's like, oh, you're a Disney fan?

00:09:05.660 --> 00:09:07.380
What was the first Mickey Mouse cartoon ever made?

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.360
It's like it doesn't have to start there.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:12.880
You know, like I've only seen The Lion King and Aladdin.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.620
Don't, you know, don't jump on me.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.160
Those movies also didn't come out yet.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:17.780
So let's focus on what would have came out.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:19.460
Sorry. I'm just a big Snow White fan.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:20.720
Don't know anything about Mickey Mouse.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:25.540
But the question itself, what was the first Mickey Mouse cartoon ever made?

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.140
There's A, Steamboat Willie.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:29.740
B, Plane Crazy.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:32.100
C, Gallop and Gaucho.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.380
And believe it or not, Steamboat Willie seems to be the obvious one.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:41.840
But it's not. It's actually Plane Crazy, the one that, like, Disney would never reference anymore today.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.460
In Plane Crazy, I think Mickey's smoking a cigarette.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:46.740
I don't even think I got that one.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.940
Maybe there's some randomness to this.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:49.840
It is randomized.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.920
Yes, it is. They have more questions, I think, than potential, like, answers or whatever.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.380
That's actually infuriating.

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.560
Because you would think that with these.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.720
Because I even got one that's just like a stat.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:02.540
Like, sorry to cut you off.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:07.180
But it's just how many Mickey Mouse cartoons are there?

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.400
And, like, this is a time before Wikipedia.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.480
And I'm sure it's not even in any fucking encyclopedia.

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:18.580
I don't know the actual number of Mickey Mouse cartoons there are.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:20.760
But it's like 169 or something.

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:21.920
I don't know the actual number.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.980
But that was a question.

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:25.600
And, like, I would have.

00:10:25.880 --> 00:10:33.260
Before you said that, I'd be like, alright, well, you know, you'll just play the game enough times that you'll guess the two wrong ones if you're not right.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:36.260
And then eventually you'll get it.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:37.780
But no, it's not even that.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:40.380
It's like you have to know that?

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:47.980
Because, like, then it'll just give you a different question that you don't know process of elimination answers on.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.260
Sorry. Go ahead. Yeah.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:50.500
No, absolutely.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:56.700
Absolutely. And, you know, the trivia thing, too, though, it just needs to be reined in a little bit.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.760
Because I do think it's too broad for Disney stuff.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:03.820
Because this is the kind of thing that, again, I have some experience with as a child.

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.560
We had a...

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:06.920
It kind of was a board game.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:08.320
But you didn't have to really play the board game.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:10.840
You could just grab the trivia cards and read the trivia questions.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.320
And there was two boxes.

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:18.280
There was a box of, like, 60 blue cards that were clearly for kids.

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:25.980
And then there was, like, 400 red cards that were, like, just the most insane things.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:37.560
It was like, what's the cat's name in some random cartoon from 1937 that, you know, was shown maybe five times ever.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.320
And, obviously, there are people who are going to know that, right?

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.140
Just like there are people who are going to know every in and out of every NES game.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.440
Maybe that'll be us by the time we're done with the podcast.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.300
Probably not for Shawnee.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.760
No, I don't even know what we played last week.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.140
Right. But my point being that, like, yes, anybody could answer those questions.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:03.420
But with trivia, especially when you're playing a video game for kids, like, you got to rein it in a little bit.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:13.080
And just say, like, if this is the same game where Space Mountain is just you pushing directions on the D-pad based on where the game tells you to go,

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:20.000
that, theoretically, the question should be of the same kind of skill set that you can just kind of follow directions, right?

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:24.600
Yeah. And this is just sort of reiterating what you've just said.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:34.960
But did you get the question, which of these non-Disney characters did not feature in Who Framed Roger Rabbit?

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:36.200
Did you get that question?

00:12:36.420 --> 00:12:37.860
I did not get that question.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:39.300
It's like a double negative.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:43.200
It's like, which of these didn't happen?

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.940
Which of these don't exist and didn't happen anyway?

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:52.700
Right. I don't really understand why that's a question, why they wrote it that way.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.700
Like, there are so many other things you could have asked us about.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:58.660
And, I mean, it ends up being Popeye.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:07.760
But... And the trivia stuff doesn't have to be that bad if it wasn't the fact that a key is hidden behind...

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:09.780
Yeah, you can't finish the game unless you do that.

00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:14.480
Right. And so, you know, for some people, it's not going to be a problem at all.

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.480
It's going to be very easy.

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:17.000
They know Disney very well.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:23.680
I don't think that, you know, probably the seven-year-old who picked up this game knows those kinds of questions.

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:26.360
Especially not the, what's the first Mickey Mouse cartoon.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:29.060
All that to say, though, that's a small portion of the game.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:39.340
And kind of cool, actually, the idea of, like, a ongoing quest while the rest of the game is just go to X attraction, complete, do Y, and then you get it.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:42.280
But there are, let's see, one, two, three, four.

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:43.940
There's five rides.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.280
I should have known that because there's six keys.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:48.540
There. There's five rides that you go on.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:58.360
Before we get into the rides and everything, I just want to go back to why the Magic Kingdom looks so dull then is because nothing else is interactable.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.860
Yeah. That's the real issue.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.160
Like, you should be able to go into those shops or something, right?

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:09.180
They even have weird, like, icons that make it look like you can buy things there.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.760
And maybe you can, and I just never figured out how.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:19.680
You cannot. That's so strange because otherwise it's just like, here's what looks like an ice cream cone, but it doesn't even look like a sign.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:25.180
It just looks like there's an ice cream cone there, which would make sense if it was a gameplay thing, but it's not.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.360
That's not important.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:30.200
Now, I can understand the park hasn't opened yet.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:32.280
I could buy that, right?

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:42.920
We got to find the six keys before the park opens or whatever, and who cares about these other kids that are here because they're, you know, maybe they're insiders too, or maybe they're part of the Mickey Mouse Club.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:57.900
But I can't get over the idea that something that is supposed to capture the excitement of Disney World, a place where literally, like, every square inch is thought of and has, like, oh, well, if that sign breaks, we have seven more of them in the basement just ready to go.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:02.280
So this way nothing's ever looked broken or out of place, and we can paint things in an instant.

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:10.940
To have your video game just look so bare makes me wonder, like, how many people from Disney actually, like, approved and saw this thing before it came out?

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:13.000
And we won't know that answer.

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:15.820
So we're going to move on to the rides themselves.

00:15:16.220 --> 00:15:18.620
The first ride I went on was Space Mountain.

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:22.480
I kind of just, I don't know if, like, because I went to Disney World on, like, Sean.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:29.600
I don't know if I just remembered whereabouts it might have been in the park or if it's just my brain always goes up and to the right.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:31.840
But that's where it was, and so that's what I did.

00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:38.420
There's no order or requirement for this kind of thing, so you can go to any of the rides and you can try and try again.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:45.820
But I went to Space Mountain unaware that, like, each ride was going to be its own sort of minigame and different.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:56.860
And I'll tell you, Space Mountain's probably not the one you want to go to first because while the concept is really easy, you just follow the track that is laid out for you.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:01.100
And I think it's supposed to be Mickey who's giving you the directions.

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:02.820
Yeah, he's telling you to do a barrel roll or something.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:09.080
Right, right. So he's telling, you know, you'll see on the screen the arrow to the right, and you're like, oh, you have to press right on the D-pad.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:13.320
And then you'll see an enemy ship appear, and you'll be like, oh, I have to fire my laser.

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.160
And that's fine, but you have to do that for a pretty long time.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.720
You have to get to, like, sector F, and you start in sector A.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:25.940
And then on top of that, it speeds up, like, the reaction times.

